'''Autonomous militias''' ({{lang-bl|milicias autónomas}}), popularly known as the '''Fedayín''' ({{Literal translation|self-sacrificers}}; from [[W:Arabic|Bayani]] فدائيين, ''fidā'iyyīn'') are [[Cantons of Balisca|cantonal]]-level {{W|militia|defense forces}} in the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]], responsible for territorial defense. They operate autonomously from within the scope of the [[Baliscan Armed Forces|Confederal Armed Forces]]. In annual surveys, the autonomous militias have consistently ranked as the institution most valued by Baliscans. 

The scope of action of each militia is a very precise demarcation within each [[Cantons of Balisca|canton]] and its respective [[Communes of Balisca|communes]]. The [[cantonal defense committee]] coordinates all these defense cadres, and received a monthly report from each of the group secretaries. These defense committees can also be organized at the [[Regions of Balisca|regional]] and [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal level]], due to the importance of communications and coordination in the defense of the [[Northern Triangle]].

Volunteers in these militias do not give the military echelon a [[W:Salute|salute]], and do not perform other formal military duties. All officers, elected, can quickly succeed one another at the head of a group, and members have the the right to discuss all orders and only apply them if they are in agreement.

==Cantonal Defense Committees==
The origin of the Autonomous Militias during the [[Baliscan Revolution]] is found in the Cantonal Defense Committees, clandestine organizational structures that were initially financed by and subordinate to the revolutionary unions and communal assemblies that were emerging to fill the void left by the state.

The essential functions of the early defense committees were twofold: logistics (arms and supplies) and internal security/intelligence. These committees were a reorganization and expansion of decentralized defense structures that had long fought against the violence and counter-revolutionary activities of the old regime.

===Revolutionary preparation===
The Baliscan Defense Committees, learning from previous failed insurrections in the region, abandoned the old tactic of simple spontaneous street fighting in favor of serious and methodical revolutionary preparation in the face of [[1918 Baliscan general strike|increasing violence]] leading up to the [[Storming of the Baliscan Cabildo|''Castejónazo'']] and the subsequent intercommunal fighting:

{{blockquote|"There can be no revolution without preparation. We have to put an end to the prejudice in favor of improvisation. This error, involving confidence in the creative instinct of the masses, has caused us to pay a heavy price. We cannot obtain by means of a process of spontaneous generation the indispensable means necessary for waging war on a State that has experience, heavy weaponry, and a greater capacity for offensive and defensive combat."}}

===Structure and Function===
The defense structure was built on the principle of absolute discretion and democratic accountability:
* '''Basic defense group (Squad):''' The basic unit had to be small to ensure secrecy and accountability, typically made up of six to seven trusted militants. Their functions were highly specialized and focused on intelligence gathering and logistics:
** '''Secretary:''' In charge of communication with the Cantonal Committee and reporting.
** '''Personal investigator (A-Sec):''' In charge of assessing the danger posed by enemies of the revolution (political opponents, former military officials, etc.).
** '''Strategic researcher (Tac):''' In charge of drafting blueprints, identifying strategic points, and developing tactics for defense.
** '''Utility researcher (Log):''' In charge of mapping public services: electricity, water, gas, and communications.
** '''Arms & supply investigator (Log-S):''' In charge of determining where to obtain and stockpile arms, money, and supplies.
* '''Coordination:''' The scope of action of each defense group was a very precise demarcation within each [[Communes of Balisca|commune or neighborhood]], indicated on a map. The Cantonal Defense Committee coordinated all these defense cadres and received a monthly (or weekly, during crises) report from each group secretary.
* '''Hierarchical defense:''' These small clandestine nuclei were the foundation of the armed force, capable of mobilizing larger secondary groups, and these, in turn, the entire people for the defense of the revolutionary territory.

